      "body": [
        "Langfuse’s October 20 stable releases, v4.65.0 and v4.66.0, address three ways telemetry can become misleading without an obvious ingestion failure: incomplete score views, inflated queue-wait measurements and altered metadata strings. The changes concern the fidelity of operational evidence rather than a new evaluation method or a demonstrated improvement in application quality.",
        "Version 4.65.0 fixes the v4 scores table so that a trace filter returns every score associated with that trace. It also stops the worker from counting earlier attempts as queue wait time. These are separate corrections: one concerns which evaluation records an operator sees, while the other concerns how retry activity is represented in a timing metric. Neither release note supplies measurements of how often these problems occurred or their size in production.",
        "Version 4.66.0 adds an OpenTelemetry fix that stores number-like metadata strings without their JSON quotes. For operators, the combined release notes suggest a focused regression checklist: inspect a trace with multiple scores, exercise a retried job and compare representative metadata values after ingestion. That checklist is editorial guidance, not a published test result. The notes do not establish a historical-data repair or a migration requirement, so teams should verify those details separately before assuming older records have changed."
